      "title": "JEE Main 2023 (12 Apr Shift 1)"
    }
  ],
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>Two circles in the first quadrant of radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;touch the coordinate axes. Each of them cuts off an intercept of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> units with the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>. Then&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is equal to ____.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "7",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Assuming a circle of radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>r</mi></math>&nbsp;which touches both the axis is given by,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p>Now given&nbsp;line&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;makes the intercept&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;the circle, so plotting the diagram we get,</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/8e1a4e96-6356-450b-b4fa-74ab2ce3d843-9927633.PNG\" style=\"width: 325px; height: 291px;\" /></p><p>Now from diagram,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></msqrt></math></p><p>Now using the formula of perpendicular distance of point from the line we get,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math></p><p>Hence, &nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>-</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math>&nbsp;</p>",
    "image": null
  },
